{"authored" [{"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}\\,+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5bde5d83c6d9bf036343499a4eb80396ce923eed98c27235f7de1d01c509a5f2" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/state.json" "file_sha256" "7bc04b4fa828f49d63d514301685b90ac2d3c8a5d8d4d717f2c6bee719f96386" "basis" 1484 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041191/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-126847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ about the point $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-126847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2x}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{(1+1)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-126847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cfc