gory" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cfcf71f70c1b410b4751983c15a5729de411405fd99cb74911845deb3c8915c"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-353917" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-354017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=2\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-354017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=2\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =(1)+(0)(x-2\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-2\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-2\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(1)}{4!}(x-2\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{4}}{24}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5) & ≈1-\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{4}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.29,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-354017" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cfcf71f70c1b410b4751983c15a5729de411405fd99cb74911845deb3c8915c"} {"question" "q-354017" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-354017" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.29"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={2}^{x}$ about $x=2$ to approximate the value of ${2}^{2.1}.$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n${2}^{2.1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+\\frac{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2.1.$ Since $2.1-2=0.1=\\frac{1}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2.1} & ≈4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2.1}≈4.29.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cfcf71f70c1b410b4751983c15a5729de411405fd99cb74911845deb3c8915c"} {"question" "q-363485" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363485" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4.29"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of ${5}^{1.3}.$ Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\n${5}^{1.3}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={5}^{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x} \\\\ f(1) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={5}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.3.$ Since $1.3-1=0.3=\\frac{3}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1.3} & ≈5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈8.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{1.3}≈8.0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073170/state.json" "file_sha256" "50e1209d4b49d542bcd1aac4ba16467ec818d2d0eac92a3218dd837577fa123d"} {"question" "q-363486" "field" nil "attribute" "quest